  ],
  "text": "NASA navigates the entire solar system using fifteen decimal places of pi. Pi goes on forever and it never repeats. That part is true and permanent. It is only the ratio between a circle's edge and its width. Every circle, always the same. People spent two thousand years grinding out more digits of it by hand. But the precision stops mattering almost immediately. Fifteen digits places a spacecraft across the solar system to within a few centimetres. Forty digits would size a circle as wide as the observable universe, accurate to one atom. Everything past that is not useful. It is only interesting. And we keep computing it anyway, into the trillions of digits, because it is there. Infinite complexity, and we ran out of things to spend it on at forty. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
